{
  "id": "c3",
  "title": "printf in Detail",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c3-l1",
      "title": "Decimals",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "%f prints a double with six decimals: 3.500000. Usually that's too many. %.2f prints two decimals, %.1f one, %.0f none. The value is rounded, not cut off.",
        "Several placeholders can go into one printf. The values follow in the same order, separated by commas.",
        "Mixing them up is a classic bug: %d with a double, or %f with an int, prints nonsense."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    double price = 2.499;",
          "    int count = 3;",
          "    printf(\"%d items at %.2f\\n\", count, price);",
          "    printf(\"total %.2f\\n\", count * price);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "3 items at 2.50",
          "total 7.50"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c3-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does printf(\"%.1f\", 2.26) print?",
          "options": [
            "2.3",
            "2.26",
            "2.2",
            "2.260000"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "%.1f rounds to one decimal place."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-l1-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does printf(\"%f\", 1.5) print?",
          "options": [
            "1.500000",
            "1.5",
            "1",
            "1.50"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Without a precision, %f always shows six decimals."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-l1-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Print the price with two decimals.",
          "code": [
            "    printf(\"___\\n\", price);"
          ],
          "options": [
            "%.2f",
            "%2f",
            "%d.2",
            "%f2"
          ],
          "answer": "%.2f",
          "explanation": "The dot and the 2 go between % and f."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-l1-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does this program print?",
          "code": [
            "#include <stdio.h>",
            "",
            "int main() {",
            "    int a = 3;",
            "    double b = 2.5;",
            "    printf(\"%d and %.1f\\n\", a, b);",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "options": [
            "3 and 2.5",
            "2.5 and 3",
            "3 and 2",
            "an error"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The values fill the placeholders in order: first the int, then the double."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-l1-e5",
          "type": "find_bug",
          "prompt": "This prints nonsense instead of 7.50. Which line is wrong?",
          "code": [
            "#include <stdio.h>",
            "",
            "int main() {",
            "    double cost = 7.5;",
            "    printf(\"%d\\n\", cost);",
            "    return 0;",
            "}"
          ],
          "answer": 4,
          "explanation": "cost is a double, so it needs %f or %.2f, not %d."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-l1-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How many values does printf(\"%d + %d = %d\\n\", ...) need after the text?",
          "options": [
            "3",
            "1",
            "2",
            "4"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "One value per placeholder."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c3-l2",
      "title": "Width and Alignment",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "A number between % and the letter sets the width: %5d prints at least 5 characters, right-aligned. %8.2f prints 8 characters with two decimals. Perfect for columns.",
        "A minus makes it left-aligned: %-10s. %s prints a string. A 0 pads with zeros: %03d prints 7 as 007.",
        "\\t jumps to the next tab stop, another way to line things up."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    printf(\"%-8s%6s\\n\", \"ITEM\", \"PRICE\");",
          "    printf(\"%-8s%6.2f\\n\", \"tea\", 1.8);",
          "    printf(\"%-8s%6.2f\\n\", \"cake\", 12.5);",
          "    printf(\"ticket %03d\\n\", 7);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "ITEM     PRICE",
          "tea       1.80",
          "cake     12.50",
          "ticket 007"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c3-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does printf(\"[%4d]\", 42) print?",
          "options": [
            "[  42]",
            "[42  ]",
            "[0042]",
            "[42]"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Width 4, right-aligned: two spaces, then 42."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does printf(\"[%-4d]\", 42) print?",
          "options": [
            "[42  ]",
            "[  42]",
            "[-42]",
            "[0042]"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The minus aligns it to the left."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-l2-e3",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does printf(\"%03d\", 5) print?",
          "options": [
            "005",
            "  5",
            "5",
            "500"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Width 3, padded with zeros."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-l2-e4",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Print a name left-aligned in 10 columns.",
          "code": [
            "    printf(\"___|\\n\", \"ada\");"
          ],
          "options": [
            "%-10s",
            "%10s",
            "%s10",
            "%-10d"
          ],
          "answer": "%-10s",
          "explanation": "%s for a string, the minus for left, 10 for the width."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-l2-e5",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Which format prints 3.14159 as \"   3.14\"?",
          "options": [
            "%7.2f",
            "%2.7f",
            "%.2f",
            "%7f"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "7 characters wide, 2 decimals."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-l2-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why line up numbers in columns?",
          "options": [
            "Tables are much easier to read",
            "It's required by C",
            "It makes the program faster",
            "It saves memory"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Reports from the PDP-11 were read on paper. Neat columns mattered."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c3-l3",
      "title": "Escape Sequences",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Some characters can't be typed into a string directly. A backslash starts an escape sequence: \\n new line, \\t tab, \\\" a quote, \\\\ a backslash.",
        "To print a percent sign with printf, write %%, because a single % starts a placeholder.",
        "\\a rang the terminal's bell. On a real teletype, a little bell inside the machine really went ding."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    printf(\"She said \\\"hi\\\"\\n\");",
          "    printf(\"100%% done\\n\");",
          "    printf(\"a\\tb\\tc\\n\");",
          "    printf(\"C:\\\\unix\\n\");",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "She said \"hi\"",
          "100% done",
          "a\tb\tc",
          "C:\\unix"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c3-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How do you print a double quote inside a string?",
          "options": [
            "\\\"",
            "\"\"",
            "'\"'",
            "%\""
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The backslash tells C the quote is part of the text, not its end."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-l3-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How do you print a % sign with printf?",
          "options": [
            "%%",
            "\\%",
            "%",
            "%p"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "A single % starts a placeholder, so printf needs two."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3-l3-e3",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Print a tab between the words.",
          "code": [
            "    printf(\"A___B\\n\");"
